  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096769",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-79299",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096769/assets/0d7b5a0f5154da800e03519b56f3d66188fa14c9f1ee636ad246c32dc2234b7b.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,14).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,14),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=14$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+4)^{2} & =4p(14+4) \\\\ 36 & =4p(18) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =2(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+4)^{2} & =2(y+4) \\\\ 16 & =2(y+4) \\\\ 8 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =8-4 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $4.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
