  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097284",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-106411",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e",
      "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097284/assets/3f1e86d5eebc6e36b4e4087dbdead613f45be89936651f529797c3faa331e99b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(6,9).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,9),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=9$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ 9=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ 3 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(1)t,\\,y=(1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=2t",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x=2t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x={t}^{2},\\,\\,y=2t",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
