  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097284",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-84738",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations",
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{6}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =6x,\\,{t}^{2}=12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =36{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=12y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2} & =12y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}y.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
